  { name: 'Somali Crow', url: '/species/somali-crow/' },
  { name: 'Diet',       url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'What does the Somali Crow eat?', a: 'The Somali Crow\'s diet has not been systematically studied. Based on its habitat (arid thorn-scrub and open country in Somalia, Djibouti, and eastern Ethiopia), close relationship to the Brown-necked Raven, and general corvid feeding ecology, it likely eats invertebrates, small vertebrates, carrion, seeds, and food waste from human settlements. Field observations are limited due to the species\' restricted and poorly accessible range.' },
  { q: 'Is the Somali Crow rare?', a: 'The Somali Crow is classified as Least Concern by IUCN, though it has a restricted range in the Horn of Africa. It is poorly studied due to the political and logistical difficulties of ornithological fieldwork in Somalia. Most knowledge of the species comes from limited museum specimens and brief field sightings. Its ecology and behaviour remain much less documented than other raven species.' },
  { q: 'Is the Somali Crow the same as the Dwarf Raven?', a: 'Yes. The Somali Crow (<em>Corvus edithae</em>) is also known as the Dwarf Raven — a name reflecting its small size compared to other ravens. It is closely related to the Brown-necked Raven (<em>Corvus ruficollis</em>) and was formerly treated as a subspecies of it. Current taxonomy (IOC) recognises it as a full species based on morphological and distributional differences.' },
];
