    <h1>Somali Crow Diet: What This Species Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Somali Crow (<em>Corvus edithae</em>), also called the Dwarf Raven, is a poorly studied species restricted to the Horn of Africa. Its diet has not been systematically documented. Based on its habitat, close relationship to the Brown-necked Raven, and general corvid ecology, it is presumed to be an omnivore exploiting invertebrates, small vertebrates, carrion, seeds, and food near human habitations in its arid range.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Is Known About the Diet</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Somali Crow is one of the least-studied birds in the world. Direct dietary data — stomach content analyses, feeding watches, or foraging studies — are essentially absent from the published literature. What can be inferred comes from:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Habitat:</strong> arid thorn-scrub, dry acacia-commiphora woodland, and open semi-desert in Somalia, Djibouti, and eastern Ethiopia. This habitat is rich in lizards, beetles, termites, and seasonal insects — typical prey of small corvids in arid zones.</li>
      <li><strong>Morphology:</strong> the Somali Crow has a relatively slender bill for a corvid — consistent with invertebrate and small-prey foraging rather than heavy carrion-opening.</li>
      <li><strong>Ecology of relatives:</strong> the Brown-necked Raven (<em>C. ruficollis</em>) from the same and adjacent arid zones eats invertebrates, lizards, carrion, seeds, dates, and human food waste. The Somali Crow likely exploits a similar dietary niche.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Research Gaps</h2>
    <p>The Somali Crow is among the corvids most urgently in need of basic ecological study. Systematic diet data, foraging observations, and habitat use studies are lacking entirely. The political situation in Somalia has made extended fieldwork impossible for decades, leaving the species almost entirely known from museum specimens and very limited field sightings. Any dietary data for this species from future fieldwork would be a valuable contribution to corvid biology.</p>
  </section>

